---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";

const card = archiveAi.idea;
const cents = 1250;
const money = (n: number) => `$${n.toLocaleString("en-US", { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 })}`;

const reports = [
  { name: "Report A", input: "checkout message", code: "same version", setting: "amounts are in dollars", value: money(cents) },
  { name: "Report B", input: "checkout message", code: "same version", setting: "amounts are in cents", value: money(cents / 100) },
];

const examples = [
  { field: "Weather monitoring", raw: "Station messages", derived: "Daily temperature chart", outcome: "Replay recovers it", tone: "is-ok", text: "A station sends readings that feed a daily temperature chart. A parser bug drops readings below freezing. Once the parser is fixed, replaying the saved station messages restores those readings to the chart." },
  { field: "Journalism", raw: "Interview recording", derived: "Transcript", outcome: "No replay recovers it", tone: "is-bad", text: "An interview recording is raw data for a transcript. If the recorder was stopped during an answer, replay can’t recover the missing audio because it was never saved. If someone later overwrites the only saved original with a trimmed copy, replay can’t recover the removed audio either." },
];

const checks = [
  ["Dropped rows", "If an import script accidentally drops rows, what would you need to have saved before the import to recover them?"],
  ["Provenance", "If two reports use the same raw inputs and code version but show different totals, what part of their provenance would you check next?"],
  ["Saved or overwritten", "A corrected parser can read an old file format. Why can it repair a report built from a saved file but fail to restore a report whose only source copy was overwritten?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("idea")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's raw-and-derived study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn="In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 2 and say what the research team needs to answer the reviewer who asks how a chart number was produced."
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">Raw data, derived data and replay</h2>
  <p><strong>Raw data</strong> is what your system receives from a source. Save an exact copy before your processing code reads it, so a bug in parsing, filtering, or conversion can’t change the only record of what arrived.</p>
  <p><strong>Derived data</strong> is what your code makes from those inputs, such as a total, a chart, or a transcript. You can delete and rebuild it if you keep the raw inputs, the version of the code, and the settings that produced it. A <strong>replay</strong> runs that processing again over the saved inputs. With corrected code or settings, it can repair old outputs.</p>

  <h2>1. Retail: two reports that disagree</h2>
  <p>A checkout sends a sale for <code>{cents.toLocaleString("en-US")}</code> cents. The saved checkout message is raw data, and the sales report is derived. One report shows <strong>{reports[0].value}</strong> and another shows <strong>{reports[1].value}</strong>. Both record the same checkout message and code version. The first records the setting “amounts are in dollars,” while the second records “amounts are in cents.” That record, called <em>provenance</em>, explains the disagreement.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card">
    <div class="pa-prov">
      {reports.map((r) => (
        <div>
          <p class="pa-prov-head"><b>{r.name}</b><strong>{r.value}</strong></p>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>Raw input</dt><dd>{r.input}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Code</dt><dd>{r.code}</dd></div>
            <div class="is-diff"><dt>Setting</dt><dd>{r.setting}</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <figcaption>Two of the three records match, so the disagreement traces to the one that differs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="pr-pull">Each output should record exactly which inputs, code version, and settings produced it.</p>

  <h2>2. Weather monitoring and journalism</h2>
  <ul class="pa-examples">
    {examples.map((e) => (
      <li>
        <p class="pa-ex-head"><b>{e.field}</b><span class:list={["pr-chip", e.tone]}>{e.outcome}</span></p>
        <p class="pa-ex-io"><span>Raw: {e.raw}</span><span>Derived: {e.derived}</span></p>
        <p>{e.text}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>In Tuesday's story, the raw data is the schedule pages Kick saved on Friday. Monday's table and Tuesday's table are both derived: each is a calculation over those pages, the parser code and its config.</p>
    <p>Dunmore's page is the reply's stopped recorder. It was never saved on Friday, so no replay recovers it. Its results still come from the visitors' saved pages, as they did on Monday.</p>
    <p>One thing to carry back to the research team's check: a cleaning script is neither raw nor derived. It is code, the reply's “version of the code”, and a number made with it can only be explained if it was kept.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>3. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-prov { display: grid; gap: 0.6rem; }
  @media (min-width: 46rem) { .pa-prov { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
  .pa-prov > div { padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-prov-head { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
  .pa-prov-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  .pa-prov-head strong { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-size: 1.4rem; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .pa-prov dl { display: grid; gap: 0.25rem; margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .pa-prov dl div { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6rem 1fr; gap: 0.5rem; padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem; border-radius: 6px; }
  .pa-prov dt { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-prov dd { margin: 0; }
  .pa-prov .is-diff { background: color-mix(in srgb, #d9822b 18%, transparent); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-examples { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .pa-examples li { padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-card); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-examples p { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-examples p:last-child { margin: 0; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ex-head { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.3rem; }
  .pa-ex-head b { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-ex-io { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.2rem 1rem; color: var(--tl-muted); font-size: 0.84rem; }
</style>
